  11:     AN ENUMERATION THAT FINDS NOTHING PROVES NOTHING ABOUT THE ESTIMATOR IT DID NOT TRY.
  12:     "None of the 33" leaves Mantel-Haenszel, Peto, Hartung-Knapp, a Bayesian posterior and
  13:     every fixed weighting a reviewer might have used, and a reader cannot tell a fabricated
  14:     number from one produced by an estimator nobody thought to run.
  15: 
  16: Two bounds close it, and both are arithmetic rather than search:
  17: 
  18:   HULL      EVERY inverse-variance pool, every Mantel-Haenszel and Peto pool, and every fixed
  19:             weighting whatsoever is a CONVEX COMBINATION of the per-trial log-effects. A convex
  20:             combination lies between the smallest and the largest. So a claimed point OUTSIDE
  21:             [min, max] cannot be ANY weighted average of those trials, under any estimator that
  22:             has ever been written or ever will be. No enumeration is involved.
  23: 
  24:   TAU-PATH  Inside the hull the hull says nothing, so the second bound is the random-effects
  25:             family itself. As tau-squared runs from 0 to infinity the inverse-variance weights
  26:             run continuously from the fixed-effect weights to EQUAL weights, and the pooled
  27:             point traces a path whose endpoints are the fixed-effect pool and the UNWEIGHTED
  28:             mean of the log-effects. A claimed point outside that path is not an
  29:             inverse-variance random-effects pool of these trials AT ANY heterogeneity.
